{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DN4TDqy1RgeRAQK7oj3yJg","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I have all the information you need. Here's a comprehensive report on the `DiffTest` struct and `RunDiffTests` function:\n\n## DiffTest Struct Definition\n\nThe complete `DiffTest` struct is defined at lines 46-55 in `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5133/pkg/testutils/difftest.go`:\n\n```go\ntype DiffTest struct {\n\tName          string\n\tArgs          []string\n\tStdin         []byte\n\tEnv           []string\n\tWorkDir       string\n\tExitCode      int\n\tNormalize     []NormalizeFunc\n\tExpectedFiles map[string][]byte\n}\n```\n\n### Field Details:\n\n- **Name**: Identifies the subtest (used with `t.Run`)\n- **Args**: Command-line arguments passed to both binaries\n- **Stdin**: Raw input bytes for the binaries. `nil` means EOF immediately; an empty non-nil slice `[]byte{}` also produces no bytes but represents an open stdin that is immediately closed\n- **Env**: Environment variables. `nil` means both binaries inherit the test process environment with `LC_ALL=C` applied as a default override. When non-nil, `KEY=VALUE` pairs are merged into the inherited environment: matching keys are overridden, new keys are added\n- **WorkDir**: Working directory for binary execution. If empty, a temporary directory is created via `t.TempDir()`\n- **ExitCode**: Expected exit code for both binaries. Defaults to 0 when left as the zero value\n- **Normalize**: A slice of `NormalizeFunc` functions applied in order to stdout and stderr of both binaries before comparison. `nil` or empty means no normalization\n- **ExpectedFiles**: A map of relative file paths to their expected byte contents. Used to verify files created by the binaries during execution\n\n## RunDiffTests Function\n\nThe function signature (lines 83-90):\n\n```go\nfunc RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tfor _, tc := range tests {\n\t\tt.Run(tc.Name, func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\t\trunSubtest(t, goBinary, refBinary, tc)\n\t\t})\n\t}\n}\n```\n\n**Purpose**: Executes each `DiffTest` as a named subtest, running the Go binary and reference binary with identical inputs and comparing their outputs.\n\n## Exit Code Comparison\n\nExit code comparison happens in the `compareOutputs` function (lines 176-185):\n\n```go\nfunc compareOutputs(t *testing.T, tc DiffTest, goRes, refRes binaryResult) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tstdoutMatch := bytes.Equal(goRes.stdout, refRes.stdout)\n\tstderrMatch := bytes.Equal(goRes.stderr, refRes.stderr)\n\texitMatch := goRes.exitCode == refRes.exitCode\n\tif stdoutMatch && stderrMatch && exitMatch {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tt.Fatalf(\"%s\", formatDivergence(tc, goRes, refRes))\n}\n```\n\nExit codes are extracted in the `extractResult` function (lines 130-148):\n- Returns `exitCode: 0` when there's no error (successful execution)\n- Extracts the actual exit code from `*exec.ExitError` using `exitErr.ExitCode()`\n- Fatals if the binary fails to start for other reasons\n\n## Stderr Comparison\n\nStderr is captured and compared alongside stdout:\n\n1. **Capture**: Both binaries' stderr is captured separately via `cmd.Stderr = &stderr` (lines 117-119 in `runBinary`)\n2. **Normalization**: Stderr is normalized along with stdout using the same `Normalize` functions (lines 101-102 in `runSubtest` via `applyNormalizers`)\n3. **Comparison**: Stderr byte equality is checked in `compareOutputs` at line 179: `stderrMatch := bytes.Equal(goRes.stderr, refRes.stderr)`\n4. **Error Report**: If stderr diverges, the formatted divergence message includes both ref and go stderr (lines 194-195 in `formatDivergence`)\n\n## ExpectedFiles Handling\n\nThe `ExpectedFiles` field is processed in the `checkExpectedFiles` function (lines 211-227):\n\n```go\nfunc checkExpectedFiles(t *testing.T, workDir string, expected map[string][]byte) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tif expected == nil {\n\t\treturn\n\t}\n\tfor relPath, want := range expected {\n\t\tfullPath := filepath.Join(workDir, relPath)\n\t\tgot, err := os.ReadFile(fullPath)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"expected file %s: %v\", relPath, err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif !bytes.Equal(got, want) {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"file %s divergence\\n  expected: %q\\n  actual:   %q\",\n\t\t\t\trelPath, want, got)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n```\n\n**How it works**:\n- If `ExpectedFiles` is `nil`, the check is skipped\n- For each relative path in the map, the function constructs the full path using the binary's `workDir`\n- Reads the file from disk and compares byte-for-byte with the expected content\n- Fatals if the file doesn't exist or if the content diverges\n\n## Helper Functions and Key Behavior\n\n**Binary Execution** (`runBinary` lines 107-128):\n- Uses `exec.CommandContext` with a default 10-second timeout\n- Captures stdout and stderr in separate buffers\n- Accepts optional stdin (if `nil`, no stdin is provided; if non-nil, it's piped to the binary)\n- Sets the working directory and environment variables\n\n**Environment Building** (`buildEnv` lines 150-166):\n- Merges test-specified environment variables with the process environment\n- Always sets `LC_ALL=C` by default\n- Test-specified variables override inherited ones\n\n**Normalizer Composition**:\n- A `NormalizeFunc` is a type alias: `func([]byte) []byte`\n- Multiple normalizers can be applied in sequence (lines 168-174)\n- A built-in `TimestampNormalizer` is provided for normalizing common timestamp formats"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"15bf8951-d47b-478d-b534-8844b96c176a","uuid":"a1a49d26-6129-4fae-b115-68b85c4959fd","timestamp":"2026-05-14T03:09:50.655Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"In the repo at /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5133, I need to understand the `DiffTest` struct and `RunDiffTests` function in `pkg/testutils/`. Specifically:\n\