e parent directory doesn't exist. I'll skip more exotic cases like `/dev/full` since that won't exist on macOS, and keep the tests focused on the core error behavior.\n\nOne thing I'm noticing is that the spec requires handling memory allocation failures gracefully, but the current Go implementation uses `io.ReadAll` which would panic on OOM rather than recovering cleanly—I should consider whether adding a `recover()` handler in main would be necessary to meet that requirement. I can use named return values so the deferred function can modify the exit code, which would handle the recovery properly. Though I'm skeptical about testing this—triggering actual OOM isn't practical, and Go's runtime panics aren't always recoverable anyway. The pragmatic approach is to add the recover handler for best-effort compliance, test with large inputs to ensure stability, and skip trying to artificially trigger OOM conditions. Now I need to integrate this into the actual `run()` function by wrapping it with a recovery mechanism.\n\nI'm realizing the defer execution order matters here—deferred functions run in LIFO order, so the cleanup handler needs to execute before the recovery handler can catch any panics. Let me reconsider the ordering to ensure cleanup happens properly even if a panic occurs. the panic is still active when the recover handler runs, so it successfully catches it and stops the program from crashing. The cleanup happens first in LIFO order, then recovery stops the panic, and the function returns with exit code 1.\n\nNow I'm modifying the `run()` function to add the defer recovery handler at the top, keeping the cleanup defer below it so cleanup runs first during unwinding. The function signature changes to return a named `exitCode` variable that the recovery handler can set, and the rest of the function stays the same since it already uses explicit returns.\n\nLet me verify the original return statements work with this approach. I'm satisfied that the recover hand